old_leakage_text = r"""Applying a strict threshold of $D_H \le 5$ (which ensures high precision for near-identical camera frames), our audit uncovered 617 near-duplicate image pairs within the public benchmark \cite{machuve2021poultry} (8,770 raw files). More critically, 316 duplicate pairs spanned partition boundaries, including 156 cross-partition train-test duplicate pairs and 45 val-test duplicate pairs. This cross-partition leakage arose from burst shooting during coop data collection, where multiple consecutive frames captured identical fecal droppings under static lighting. Sweeping the threshold up to $D_H \le 8\text{--}10$ reveals broader perceptual clusters with subtle lighting shifts, but $D_H \le 5$ strictly isolates unmistakable frame duplicates without discarding distinct visual manifestations.

To quantify the empirical effect size of this partition contamination, we evaluated MobileNetV2 across isolated test subsets (Table~\ref{tab:leakage}). On the 156 leaked duplicate pairs, MobileNetV2 achieved 98.08\% top-1 accuracy, correctly classifying 100\% of Coccidiosis and 100\% of Healthy samples. In contrast, on the non-leaked test subset ($n=1,598$), accuracy was 97.93\%. Across the full partition, top-1 accuracy on the original published split (97.95\%) is only 0.03 percentage points higher than on the decontaminated sterile benchmark (97.92\%). This establishes an honest empirical finding: while duplicate frames themselves exhibit near-perfect recognition, the net diagnostic inflation on overall top-1 accuracy in this specific dataset is minor ($\Delta \approx +0.03\%$). Nonetheless, removing duplicate pairs to construct a clean benchmark ($N=8,153$) is essential methodological hygiene, ensuring that edge compression benchmarks reflect genuine generalization on unseen livestock droppings rather than artifactual memorization."""

new_leakage_text = r"""Applying a strict threshold of $D_H \le 5$ (which ensures high precision for near-identical camera frames), our audit uncovered 617 near-duplicate image pairs within the public benchmark \cite{dianyo2022fecal} (8,770 raw files). More critically, 316 duplicate pairs spanned partition boundaries, including 156 cross-partition train-test duplicate pairs. This cross-partition leakage arose from burst shooting during coop data collection, where multiple consecutive frames captured identical fecal droppings under static lighting. Sweeping the threshold up to $D_H \le 8\text{--}10$ reveals broader burst clusters with subtle sensor noise and illumination shifts; $D_H \le 5$ conservatively isolates unmistakable frame twins without discarding distinct visual manifestations. Decontamination was executed by preserving the published test partition intact ($n=1,728$ test samples after purging 26 unreadable or pixel-identical files) and purging the 156 duplicate counterparts from the training partition, creating an 8,153-image sterile dataset ($N=8,153$) where no test sample has a training twin.

To quantify the empirical effect size of this partition contamination, we evaluated MobileNetV2 across isolated test subsets (Table~\ref{tab:leakage}). On the 156 leaked duplicate pairs, MobileNetV2 achieved 98.08\% top-1 accuracy, correctly classifying 100\% of Coccidiosis and 100\% of Healthy samples. In contrast, on the non-leaked test subset ($n=1,598$), accuracy was 97.93\% (+0.15 pp optimistic bias on duplicates). Across the full partition, top-1 accuracy on the original published split (97.95\%) is only 0.03 percentage points higher than on the decontaminated sterile benchmark (97.92\%). This establishes an honest empirical finding: while duplicate frames themselves exhibit slight over-fitting, near-duplicate leakage did not produce artificial metric inflation in published baseline CNNs. Decontamination represents necessary scientific hygiene rather than correcting a distorted baseline."""

assert old_leakage_text in text, "old_leakage_text not found"
