# 6. Section V.B (Statistical Significance & McNemar tests)
old_mcnemar = r"""To evaluate whether the performance difference between naive dynamic INT8 and static per-channel QDQ calibration is statistically significant, we performed a paired McNemar test on the sterile test split ($n=1,728$). Dynamic INT8 correctly classified only 180 samples (12 Coccidiosis, 24 Healthy, 141 Newcastle Disease, and 3 Salmonella), whereas static QDQ correctly classified 1,634 samples. The discordant cell counts are $b = 14$ (samples correctly classified by dynamic INT8 but missed by static QDQ) and $c = 1,468$ (samples misclassified by dynamic INT8 but correctly resolved by static QDQ). Applying the continuity-corrected McNemar test statistic:
\begin{equation}
\chi^2 = \frac{(|b - c| - 1)^2}{b + c} = \frac{(|14 - 1468| - 1)^2}{14 + 1468} = 1424.57
\end{equation}
yielding $p < 10^{-15}$, confirming that the recovery achieved by static per-channel QDQ calibration is highly statistically significant. Furthermore, comparing static QDQ against the unquantized FP32 baseline (1,703 correct predictions, discordant counts $b = 75, c = 7$) yields a paired McNemar test statistic of $\chi^2 = 54.74$ ($p = 1.37 \times 10^{-13}$), demonstrating that while per-channel QDQ completely prevents catastrophic mode collapse, the minor 3.99\% accuracy delta reflects slight quantization variance on subtle pathological features rather than structural model degradation."""

new_mcnemar = r"""To evaluate whether the performance difference between naive dynamic INT8 and static per-channel QDQ calibration is statistically significant, we performed paired McNemar tests (with Edwards continuity correction) on the sterile test split ($n=1,728$). Dynamic INT8 correctly classified only 180 samples (12 Coccidiosis, 24 Healthy, 141 Newcastle Disease, and 3 Salmonella), whereas static QDQ correctly classified 1,634 samples. The discordant cell counts are $b = 14$ (samples correctly classified by dynamic INT8 but missed by static QDQ) and $c = 1,468$ (samples misclassified by dynamic INT8 but correctly resolved by static QDQ). Applying the continuity-corrected McNemar test statistic:
\begin{equation}
\chi^2 = \frac{(|b - c| - 1)^2}{b + c} = \frac{(|14 - 1468| - 1)^2}{14 + 1468} = 1424.57
\end{equation}
yielding $p < 10^{-15}$, confirming that the recovery achieved by static per-channel QDQ calibration is highly statistically significant. Comparing EfficientNet-B0 static QDQ against its FP32 baseline (1,703 correct predictions, discordant counts $b = 75, c = 7$) yields $\chi^2 = 54.74$ ($p = 1.37 \times 10^{-13}$), reflecting slight quantization variance on subtle pathological features rather than structural degradation.

Critically, on MobileNetV2 and ShuffleNetV2, static per-channel QDQ performs statistically identically to baseline FP32. On MobileNetV2, discordant counts between FP32 and Static QDQ are $b=9, c=3$, yielding $\chi^2 = \frac{(|9-3|-1)^2}{9+3} = 2.0833$ ($p = 0.1489 > 0.05$); on ShuffleNetV2, discordant counts are $b=3, c=1$, yielding $\chi^2 = 0.25$ ($p = 0.6171 > 0.05$). Neither architecture exhibits statistically significant accuracy loss under static QDQ. Furthermore, 10,000-sample bootstrap confidence intervals demonstrate high statistical stability: MobileNetV2 overall accuracy is 97.74\% [Bootstrap 95\% CI: 97.00\%, 98.38\%], and ShuffleNetV2 is 97.57\% [96.82\%, 98.26\%]. The Wilson 95\% CI for Newcastle Disease recall ([89.20\%, 97.10\%]) indicates that while the point estimate remains robust at 94.33\% (133/141), the sample size of $n=141$ naturally encompasses values below 90.0\%, requiring transparent disclosure in safety-critical deployments."""

assert old_mcnemar in text, "old_mcnemar not found"
text = text.replace(old_mcnemar, new_mcnemar)

