old_pareto_text = r"""Fig.~\ref{fig:pareto} illustrates the multi-objective Pareto trade-off frontiers. Because the Raspberry Pi 5 possesses 8\,GB of RAM and peak runtime RSS across all models is strictly bounded between 73.16\,MB and 103.38\,MB, disk storage footprint does not constrain operational deployment. Evaluating Pareto optimality across Accuracy, NCD Recall, and Execution Latency demonstrates that \textbf{MobileNetV2 Baseline Static QDQ} (7.59\,ms, 131.8 FPS, 97.74\% accuracy, 94.33\% NCD recall [89.20\%, 97.10\%]) and \textbf{ShuffleNetV2 Baseline Static QDQ} (6.29\,ms, 159.1 FPS, 97.57\% accuracy, 94.33\% NCD recall [89.20\%, 97.10\%]) unequivocally dominate the deployment frontier. They outperform their uncompressed FP32 counterparts in hardware throughput by 1.33$\times$ to 2.67$\times$ while matching baseline diagnostic accuracy (-0.12\% to -0.18\%) and maintaining identical point-estimate pathogen sensitivity. For compound depthwise architectures where dynamic quantization fails, \textbf{EfficientNet-B0 Static QDQ INT8} (19.44\,ms, 51.5 FPS, 4.67\,MB, 94.56\% accuracy, 92.91\% NCD recall [87.44\%, 96.10\%]) delivers a 2.07$\times$ speedup over FP32 (40.19\,ms) while restoring diagnostic viability. Consequently, static per-channel QDQ represents the universally optimal deployment strategy across all evaluated architectures."""

